VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-046
Reason for Visit: New, excruciating headache on one side of the head and pain in the jaw when eating.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a new-onset, severe localized headache in an elderly patient, accompanied by jaw pain during chewing (jaw claudication). This combination is highly specific for Giant Cell Arteritis (GCA), a medical emergency carrying a profound risk of irreversible, sudden, and permanent blindness. Urgent physical assessment was strictly required to palpate the temporal arteries, check visual acuity, and initiate immediate high-dose corticosteroid therapy, precluding any delay until routine hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The side of my head is throbbing so badly I can't sleep, my scalp burns when I brush my hair, and my jaw aches terribly when I try to chew."
History of Present Illness: Patient (aged 72) reports a 5-day history of a new-onset severe localized headache in the right temporal region. Reports exquisite scalp tenderness, notably when brushing hair or resting on a pillow. Crucially, the patient describes jaw claudication, characterized by severe pain and fatigue of the masseter muscles during chewing, which resolves when resting the jaw. Reports systemic symptoms including profound fatigue, a low-grade fever, and a 2kg unintentional weight loss over the past two weeks. Denies any sudden painless transient or permanent visual loss in either eye at this stage. KEY SYMPTOMS: New-onset severe localized headache. Scalp tenderness notably when brushing hair. Jaw claudication characterized by pain or fatigue of the masseter muscles during chewing. Systemic symptoms such as profound fatigue, low-grade fever.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently distressed and unable to eat solid food due to jaw pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of sudden visual loss without immediate intervention.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98 percent | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, holding the right side of the head, mildly febrile.
Physical Exam: HEENT: The right superficial temporal artery is hardened, prominent, visibly nodular, and exquisitely tender to light palpation. The right temporal artery pulse is palpably diminished compared to the left. Visual fields are fully intact to confrontation, and visual acuity is grossly preserved (reading a newspaper at 30cm). No relative afferent pupillary defect. Musculoskeletal: Mild proximal muscle stiffness noted in the shoulders (Polymyalgia rheumatica overlap). Cardiovascular: Normal heart sounds, no carotid or subclavian bruits. DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: New-onset temporal headache, Jaw claudication, Scalp tenderness, Elevated ESR/CRP (anticipated massively elevated on urgent morning bloods).
Environmental Observations: Half-eaten meal on the table, abandoned due to jaw pain.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking paracetamol with absolutely no relief of the headache.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Giant Cell Arteritis (Temporal Arteritis) - Status: Acute (High Risk of Vision Loss)
Differentials:

Tension Headache - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a hardened, tender, pulseless temporal artery, the specific symptom of jaw claudication, and the new onset in an elderly patient.

Trigeminal Neuralgia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pain is a continuous, severe throbbing ache associated with systemic symptoms (fever, fatigue) rather than sharp, brief, electric shock-like spasms triggered by touch.

Temporomandibular Joint (TMJ) Dysfunction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: TMJ dysfunction lacks the systemic inflammatory signs, scalp tenderness, and specifically the hardened temporal artery; jaw claudication in GCA is ischemic fatigue, not joint mechanical clicking.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Risk of sudden blindness)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the pathognomonic clinical picture of Giant Cell Arteritis: a new, severe temporal headache, jaw claudication, scalp tenderness, and a visibly inflamed, pulseless temporal artery. This is an absolute ophthalmic and rheumatologic emergency. Because the inflammatory process can abruptly occlude the ophthalmic artery causing permanent blindness, an urgent after-hours physical assessment was critical to confirm the diagnosis clinically and administer the first dose of high-dose corticosteroids immediately, rather than waiting for morning blood tests or a routine appointment.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Prednisolone 60mg orally immediately on-site. Arranged urgent fasting bloods for the morning (ESR, CRP, Full Blood Count). Generated an urgent referral to Rheumatology and Ophthalmology for a formal temporal artery biopsy and visual assessment.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instructed the patient to immediately switch to a completely soft or liquid diet for the next 48 hours. By eliminating the mechanical effort of chewing, the patient removes the ischemic demand on the inflamed jaw muscles. The reward is a rapid reduction in the agonizing jaw claudication while the high-dose steroids begin to reduce the systemic inflammation.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: None.
Follow-up: Urgent General Practice review within 24 hours to review inflammatory markers and confirm specialist appointments.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient experiences any sudden blurring of vision, a "curtain" falling over their eye, or a complete loss of vision in one or both eyes, they must call an ambulance immediately for emergency transport to an eye hospital.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive based on classic clinical signs; temporal artery biopsy is required for definitive confirmation; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: GCA requires immediate, high-dose steroid treatment to prevent blindness. Treatment is usually required for 1-2 years, with doses slowly tapering down as inflammation subsides.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Take the high-dose steroids exactly as prescribed. Do not chew hard foods. Seek emergency care immediately if you experience any change in your vision.

